        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: क्षम्यताम्",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "क्षम्यताम् (kṣamyatām) — \"pardon me; let it be forgiven\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "क्षम्यताम् (kṣamyatām) is the apology. It is longer than sorry and it is doing more work: the root means \"to be patient, to bear with, to forgive\", and the form is a command addressed to nobody in particular. Let it be forgiven."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Notice that Sanskrit has now given you three of these impersonal commands: अस्तु, \"let it be\", क्षम्यताम् (kṣamyatām), \"let it be forgiven\", and स्वस्ति, \"may it be well\". A polite language often prefers to leave the person out."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "An apology that never names who should do the forgiving."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
