    {
      "id": "SA-R29-second-pass-how-this-book-argues",
      "sequence": 1425,
      "title": "धन्यः (dhanyaḥ) — the ending you can see, and the argument underneath it",
      "headword": "धन्यः",
      "romanization": "dhanyaḥ",
      "gloss": "second pass over the visarga, two signs read inside words you say, and the four claims this book makes about where its words come from",
      "script": "devanagari",
      "modality": "sight",
      "derivedModality": "sight",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "script-block"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:825accb4e91d2826",
      "notice": {
        "modality": "sight",
        "needs": [
          "your eyes, for letter shapes on the page"
        ],
        "waitUntilStopped": [
          "Script: two marks, read inside words you already say"
        ],
        "text": "Before we start: this one needs your eyes, so it is not fully a driving lesson. You will want your eyes, for letter shapes on the page. You can listen to everything else now — leave the section called Script: two marks, read inside words you already say until you have stopped, and I will say so again when we reach it."
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Three of this chapter's words end on the same soft breath — praṇāmaḥ, upakāraḥ, dhanyaḥ. Say what that breath marks. Then say which of the three names the bow you have been making since your first greeting."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "script",
          "title": "Script: two marks, read inside words you already say",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Cold, with nothing to help you but the shapes."
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "READ",
              "instruction": "वैद्यः — name the double stroke above the top bar, and say what it replaced",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU READ: **वैद्यः** — name the double stroke above the top bar, and say what it replaced]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "READ",
              "instruction": "इदानीम् — name its opening letter, and say why that vowel is full-size here",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU READ: **इदानीम्** — name its opening letter, and say why that vowel is full-size here]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One of the two is a sign that cannot stand alone and replaces the a inside a consonant. The other is the full-size letter a vowel wears when it opens a word and has nothing to lean on. Same vowel sound, two jobs, two shapes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "culture-pragmatics",
          "title": "Why it's said this way",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Every claim this book has made about where a word came from has used the same move: cut the word at its seam, and compare the pieces."
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "the claim",
                "the seam it cuts on"
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 4,
              "utterances": [
                "the claim: Sanskrit, Greek and Latin share a source. the seam it cuts on: verb endings, not vocabulary lists.",
                "the claim: the naming words have cousins west of India. the seam it cuts on: nāma, mama, tvam, kim.",
                "the claim: the wellbeing words reach east and west alike. the seam it cuts on: the root travels; the finished word does not.",
                "the claim: leave-taking is built from parts you can name. the seam it cuts on: again plus for seeing."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
